{"$defs":{},"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claimed mechanism—that four straight cuts through the center leave the pizza as one piece—relies on an open-set/topological interpretation that explicitly excludes boundaries. But that interpretation cannot actually yield one piece for four distinct concurrent lines: removing four distinct diameters (minus the center) from an open disk does not leave it path-connected; each new diameter further separates sectors, producing eight connected components, not one. The only way to keep the disk connected is to have the cuts degenerate/overlap so there is effectively only one diameter, which contradicts the problem's 'four straight cuts' as distinct lines through the center.","counterexample":["Two distinct straight cuts through the center are two distinct diameters; removing both from the open disk yields four disjoint open sectors, so it is already not one path-connected piece—contradicting the claim that four cuts leave exactly one connected piece."],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}